WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.360
Converting pyruvate to acetyl CoA is not one reaction but three, carried out in

2
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.120
sequence by a single complex.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:17.720
First comes decarboxylation, where pyruvate loses carbon dioxide, and the remaining

4
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.400
two-carbon fragment is captured on thiamine pyrophosphate.

5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.140
Second is oxidation, where two electrons leave and the fragment is handed to the

6
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.460
lipoamide arm. Third is transfer to coenzyme A, producing acetyl CoA.

7
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.680
Follow the carbon across the three boxes and follow the electrons separately,

8
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.440
because those electrons end up reducing NAD⁺ plus to NADH.

9
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.900
Notice that each step uses its own coenzyme, and several of those coenzymes trace

10
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.000
back to B vitamins.

11
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:51.683
Watch the lipoamide arm make its rounds.

12
00:00:52.363 --> 00:00:57.263
Tethered to the E2 core, it first swings to E1 and picks up the acetyl group,

13
00:00:57.583 --> 00:00:59.263
becoming acetyl lipoamide.

14
00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:05.183
Next, it pivots back to E2 and delivers that acetyl unit to coenzyme A, releasing

15
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:09.843
acetyl CoA and leaving the arm reduced as dihydrolipoamide.

16
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:16.703
Finally, it reaches E3 to be reoxidized, regenerating the disulfide so the cycle can

17
00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:23.083
repeat. Because the arm is about 14 angstroms long and the E2 core has many copies,

18
00:01:23.563 --> 00:01:28.723
24 or 60 depending on the organism, all three active sites stay within reach.

19
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:30.903
The intermediates never escape.

20
00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:33.823
They ride the tethered arm from station to station.

21
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:39.147
Let me put the whole mechanism on one timeline so you know exactly where NADH is

22
00:01:39.147 --> 00:01:46.167
born. Step 1, decarboxylation on E1, releases carbon dioxide and makes no NADH.

23
00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:51.647
Step 2, oxidative transfer, hands the hydroxyethyl group to lipoamide as it is

24
00:01:51.647 --> 00:01:55.027
oxidized to an acetyl group, still no NADH.

25
00:01:55.387 --> 00:02:01.787
Step 3, acetyl transfer on E2, produces acetyl-CoA and leaves dihydrolipoamide,

26
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:08.767
again, no NADH. Only in step 4, regeneration on E3, is the reduced lipoamide

27
00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:13.727
reoxidized by FAD and the electrons flow onward to make NADH.

28
00:02:14.187 --> 00:02:19.287
So three steps build the product and exactly one step, the last, generates the

29
00:02:19.287 --> 00:02:20.287
reducing power.

30
00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:26.650
Enzyme E1 is the key regulatory site of the entire complex, and it is controlled by

31
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.350
a simple on-off switch built from a kinase and a phosphatase that travel with the

32
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.630
complex. A kinase phosphorylates E1 and inactivates it.

33
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:39.310
A phosphatase removes that phosphate and reactivates it.

34
00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.770
The switch reads the cell's energy state.

35
00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:48.590
When ATP, acetyl-CoA and NADH are abundant, signaling plenty of fuel and product,

36
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.550
the complex is inhibited.

37
00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:56.710
When ADP and pyruvate accumulate, signaling demand and available substrate, the

38
00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.050
complex is stimulated.

39
00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:03.550
So the gateway from glycolysis to the citric acid cycle opens only when the cell

40
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.510
actually needs to burn pyruvate.
